1
00:00:00,152 --> 00:00:03,112
Here is a question for a language model. What

2
00:00:03,112 --> 00:00:06,092
is the capital of India? You type it, you

3
00:00:06,092 --> 00:00:08,922
send it, and an answer comes back. It looks

4
00:00:08,922 --> 00:00:11,611
as though the model is reading your words directly.

5
00:00:11,724 --> 00:00:14,664
It is not. Before the model sees anything at

6
00:00:14,664 --> 00:00:17,664
all, that sentence is taken apart and turned into

7
00:00:17,664 --> 00:00:21,974
something else entirely. Watch what the model actually receives.

8
00:00:21,974 --> 00:00:23,411
A list of integers.

9
00:00:23,772 --> 00:00:26,442
One number for every piece of the text. No

10
00:00:26,442 --> 00:00:32,412
letters, no words, no punctuation, just positions in a

11
00:00:32,412 --> 00:00:34,371
table.

12
00:00:35,375 --> 00:00:38,495
So how does text become numbers? That is what

13
00:00:38,495 --> 00:00:39,898
this episode is about.

14
00:00:40,825 --> 00:00:44,225
Start from the simplest idea. A tokenizer needs a

15
00:00:44,225 --> 00:00:48,203
dictionary. On the left, readable text. On the right,

16
00:00:48,203 --> 00:00:51,245
a list of integers. The dictionary is the bridge

17
00:00:51,245 --> 00:00:52,558
between the two worlds.

18
00:00:52,759 --> 00:00:57,168
Take a short greeting: Hello, with an exclamation mark.

19
00:00:57,168 --> 00:00:59,809
The tokenizer does not keep it whole. It cuts

20
00:00:59,809 --> 00:01:03,299
it into two pieces. Hello, and the exclamation mark

21
00:01:03,299 --> 00:01:06,544
on its own. Each piece is called a token.

22
00:01:06,699 --> 00:01:09,732
Every token then looks itself up in the dictionary.

23
00:01:09,732 --> 00:01:13,119
Hello is entry forty two. The exclamation mark is

24
00:01:13,119 --> 00:01:16,139
entry ninety one. Those two integers are what the

25
00:01:16,139 --> 00:01:19,719
model receives. I invented these particular numbers to keep

26
00:01:19,719 --> 00:01:22,719
the picture clean, but the mechanism is exactly what

27
00:01:22,719 --> 00:01:24,504
every real tokenizer does.

28
00:01:25,157 --> 00:01:28,868
For a long time, the dictionary held whole words.

29
00:01:28,868 --> 00:01:31,217
Every word the system knew got its own drawer

30
00:01:31,217 --> 00:01:37,388
in the cabinet. Play. Player. Playing. Played. Four drawers,

31
00:01:37,388 --> 00:01:38,671
four entries, for one idea.

32
00:01:38,838 --> 00:01:41,888
That works beautifully until somebody uses a word that

33
00:01:41,888 --> 00:01:45,368
was never in the training data. Playfulness. There is

34
00:01:45,368 --> 00:01:48,678
no drawer for it. The searchlight sweeps the cabinet

35
00:01:48,678 --> 00:01:51,961
and finds nothing. So the word is thrown away,

36
00:01:51,961 --> 00:01:55,778
and replaced by a single marker: unknown token. The

37
00:01:55,778 --> 00:01:58,828
word is gone. The model cannot tell you what

38
00:01:58,828 --> 00:01:59,638
it was.

39
00:02:00,466 --> 00:02:02,996
The book makes exactly this point with a different

40
00:02:02,996 --> 00:02:09,806
family: apology, apologize, apologetic, apologist. Four whole words, all

41
00:02:09,806 --> 00:02:12,916
built on one stem, and the vocabulary pays for

42
00:02:12,916 --> 00:02:15,596
every one of them separately. That is a lot

43
00:02:15,596 --> 00:02:18,004
of storage for very little difference.

44
00:02:18,092 --> 00:02:20,542
But look closely at those drawers, and a pattern

45
00:02:20,542 --> 00:02:25,732
appears. The same letters keep coming back. Play. Play.

46
00:02:25,732 --> 00:02:29,692
Play. If those letters could be shared, one entry

47
00:02:29,692 --> 00:02:30,531
would do the work of many.

48
00:02:31,139 --> 00:02:35,461
So here is one escape route. Forget words entirely.

49
00:02:35,461 --> 00:02:39,259
Treat every single character as a token. Playful shatters

50
00:02:39,259 --> 00:02:43,709
into seven separate pieces. P. L. A. Y. F.

51
00:02:43,709 --> 00:02:44,631
U. L.

52
00:02:44,852 --> 00:02:48,672
Now nothing is ever unknown again, because every word

53
00:02:48,672 --> 00:02:51,652
in every language is built from characters the system

54
00:02:51,652 --> 00:02:55,092
already has. But look at the price. The word

55
00:02:55,092 --> 00:02:58,122
play, which used to be one token, is now

56
00:02:58,122 --> 00:02:58,931
four.

57
00:02:59,099 --> 00:03:02,056
And that price is paid in the model's context.

58
00:03:02,056 --> 00:03:05,299
Subword tokens average about three characters each, so a

59
00:03:05,299 --> 00:03:08,219
context of one thousand and twenty four tokens holds

60
00:03:08,219 --> 00:03:11,151
roughly three times as much text as characters would.

61
00:03:11,398 --> 00:03:14,748
Character tokenization is not wrong. It is a different

62
00:03:14,748 --> 00:03:18,588
trade: complete coverage, bought with much longer sequences for

63
00:03:18,588 --> 00:03:19,984
the model to work through.

64
00:03:20,519 --> 00:03:23,399
So the machine takes the middle path. Keep whole

65
00:03:23,399 --> 00:03:25,929
words when they are common. Break the rare ones

66
00:03:25,929 --> 00:03:31,009
into reusable pieces. Play, player, playing. All three share

67
00:03:31,009 --> 00:03:32,178
the same opening.

68
00:03:32,404 --> 00:03:36,781
That shared opening collapses into a single block: play.

69
00:03:36,781 --> 00:03:40,454
Now the endings can be stored separately. Er. And

70
00:03:40,454 --> 00:03:44,218
ing. Three words, built from three pieces.

71
00:03:44,853 --> 00:03:48,696
And a word the vocabulary has never seen? Playful.

72
00:03:48,696 --> 00:03:51,223
It needs no new entry at all. Play is

73
00:03:51,223 --> 00:03:54,553
already there. Ful is already there. The word is

74
00:03:54,553 --> 00:03:55,731
simply assembled.

75
00:03:55,970 --> 00:03:59,390
That is what the book calls subword tokenization. It

76
00:03:59,390 --> 00:04:03,800
is why the pieces for apology, apologize, apologetic and

77
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:07,710
apologist can be shared, instead of stored whole. Whole

78
00:04:07,710 --> 00:04:10,551
words when useful. Smaller pieces when needed.
